---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';

const title = 'Methodology: Formulas Behind Every Calculator | RaceCalc';
const description =
  'Every formula used by RaceCalc tools with worked examples: FOV projection, triple screen geometry, bezel correction, PPD and fuel planning maths.';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Methodology' },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} canonical="/methodology/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <h1>Methodology &amp; editorial policy</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The exact maths behind every RaceCalc calculator, written so you can verify or replicate any
      number we show.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">Last updated: 08 Oct 2026</p>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="formulas">Core formulas</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Horizontal FOV</strong> = <code>2 × atan( screen width ÷ (2 × eye distance) )</code></li>
        <li><strong>Vertical FOV</strong> = <code>2 × atan( tan(hFOV ÷ 2) ÷ aspect )</code></li>
        <li><strong>Screen width from diagonal</strong> = <code>diagonal × aspect ÷ √(aspect² + 1)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Triple total FOV</strong> = <code>per-screen FOV + 2 × side screen angle</code></li>
        <li><strong>Bezel-corrected FOV</strong> = <code>2 × atan( (3 × width + bezels) ÷ (2 × distance) )</code></li>
        <li><strong>PPD</strong> = <code>horizontal pixels ÷ horizontal FOV</code></li>
        <li><strong>Fuel per lap (with margin)</strong> = <code>practice consumption × (1 + margin%)</code></li>
        <li><strong>Safe laps per tank</strong> = <code>floor( tank ÷ fueled consumption )</code></li>
        <li><strong>Stints</strong> = <code>ceil( race laps ÷ safe laps per tank )</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        All tools implement exactly these equations; the test suite recomputes them against
        hand-verified values (for example: a 27" 16:9 screen at 60 cm subtends a horizontal FOV of about
        52.6°, verified in both the calculator and the tests).
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="sources">Sources &amp; conventions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Projection maths:</strong> standard perspective camera projection (the same model used in real-time graphics; see any computer graphics text on gluPerspective-style transforms).</li>
        <li><strong>Triple screen geometry:</strong> planar arc model as used by sim racing rig guides; the seam-free condition is side angle = per-screen FOV, which matches the arrangement recommended across the community.</li>
        <li><strong>Per-game FOV notes:</strong> based on the games' own settings documentation and community verification; we re-check each note when sims change their interfaces and log updates in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>PPD reference bands:</strong> described as practical guidance, not measured thresholds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small muted">
        Where a game hides its FOV behind an undocumented slider (some console titles), we say so on the
        tool page rather than inventing a mapping.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="limits">Known limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Curved screens</strong> subtend slightly less angle than the same width flat; treat the flat-screen number as an upper bound.</li>
        <li><strong>Side screen angles</strong> in our total FOV use the simple planar model; extreme wraparound rigs (angle &gt; 70°) distort, as do the images themselves.</li>
        <li><strong>Fuel planning</strong> assumes stable consumption; wet races, traffic and saving change it, which is what the margin is for.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="testing">Testing &amp; QA</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Every formula has a hand-calculation test case that must pass before deploy.</li>
        <li>Worked examples on pages are generated from the same functions the tools run.</li>
        <li>Per-game notes change only with a changelog entry and a source.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="ai">AI use disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        Pages are drafted with tooling assistance and reviewed before publishing; every formula and
        claim is verified against the sources above. No invented statistics appear on this site, and
        anything unverifiable is labelled or excluded.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections policy</h2>
      <p>
        Found an error? <a href="/contact/">Report it</a> with a source. Confirmed corrections ship
        within a week, are listed in the <a href="/changelog/">changelog</a>, and credit is given where
        the reporter wants it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
